# rm907, the hen-namer (Docs/Design/84). Exported from ridgemere3 at revision 1: edit it in Atlas or the DM, or as a draft through tools/mantle_load.py.
mantle rm907
revision 1
state ready
dialect plain
accent none
tier spare
written_by claude
title the hen-namer
fits stage=young|adolescent|adult|old
prefers talk=chatty warmth=warm
traits temper=even talk=chatty warmth=warm humour=merry honesty=plain piety=ordinary drive=content
quirk names_every_hen_in_the_market_coops
likes brown_eggs geese_on_the_march market_mornings warm_kitchens
dislikes cramped_cages being_hurried
fears a_fox_loose_in_the_lanes
hopes a_yard_with_a_coop_of_my_own
topics animals +0.5 food +0.4 smalltalk +0.3 day +0.2 gossip +0.2 crime -0.3
styles comfort=company gossip=eager refuse=soft tease=merry
marks oath saying
persona #self.first# chatters to anyone standing near the market coops, has a name for every hen in the coops, laughs at most things, and would sooner share an egg than sell one.

words
oath! = Feathers and fiddlesticks | Eggs and onions | Combs and crumbs
saying! = A loud hen lays as well as a quiet one | Every coop has its queen | Never name what you mean to eat
coops = the market coops | the coops by the stalls
speckle = the speckled one | old speckle, the bossy one
brownhen = the little brown hen | the brown one with the bent comb
duchess = the duchess, that big white hen | the white one I call the duchess
egg = a brown egg | an egg still warm from the straw
geese = the market geese | the geese off the river carts
march = marching like the Watch on parade | in a line, chins up, proud as anything
crumbs = a crumb for the hens | the crust in my pocket
kitchen = a warm kitchen | a kitchen with the oven going
yard = a yard of my own | a little yard with a coop at the bottom
fox = a fox | a fox come in off the hills
feathers = feathers everywhere | a feather on my sleeve again

any
opinion @1 merchants for 1 : Stallholders are my sort. Shout a price, laugh at the answer, and everybody goes home with #egg#.
opinion @1 festivals for 2 : A festival's the one day somebody ties a ribbon on a goose. I'm for anything that does that.
opinion @1 newcomers for 1 : New faces always stop at #coops# and ask the hens' names. I always tell them. Every one.
opinion @1 watch mixed 1 : The Watch walk past the stalls like #geese#, all boots and chins. Useful, I'm sure. Loud, though.
opinion @1 city for 2 : A hundred stalls and forty hens in one square. Show me another place with that. You can't.

grown
line @1 greet [pair.band=strangers] : #greet#. Here's a face that wants to hear a hen's name, I can tell. I've forty. | #greet#, #address#. Stand by #coops# with me; the wind's kinder here.
line @2 greet [here.time=dawn] : Morning. The hens beat me up again; they always do. | Morning, #address#. The first egg's laid already, and not by me.
line @1 kin.open [pair.kin!=no] : There you are. I've a crust in my pocket and a story from the coops. | You'll never guess what #speckle# did this morning. I'll tell you anyway.
line @1 again.open : Twice in a day. The hens will start talking about us. | Back so soon, and me with nothing new but a feather.
line @1 greet.return : #greet#. Lovely to see somebody who doesn't cluck. | #greet#, #address#. I was just this minute thinking about eggs. | #greet#. Come out of the draught and talk to me.
line @1 farewell : #farewell#. Say good day to any hens you pass. | Off you go, then. Mind #geese# on the corner.
line @1 farewell.return : #farewell#, #address#. Come by the coops again soon. | #farewell#. Go careful, and eat something warm.
line @1 react.news.new : #oath#! Not a word of that reached the coops. | Well, there's a thing. I'll have told it twice by noon.
line @1 react.news.known : Heard that at the stalls before the hens were fed. | That's been round the market twice, love.
line @1 react.news.doubt : Seeing's believing, and I've seen nothing of the sort. | That's the sort of tale a goose would honk at you.
line @1 react.news.sorrow [topic.kind=death|loss|theft|assault|arrest|crime] : Oh, that's hard. That's very hard to hear. | That's a sad thing to have going round the market.
line @1 react.news.glad [topic.kind=marriage|birth|apprentice|succession|work] : Oh, lovely! I'll want every bit of that. | Best news since #brownhen# laid a double yolk.
line @1 answer.how : Chirpy, thanks. Somebody has to be. | Well enough. Fed, warm and talking, which is all I ever ask. | Grand. #speckle.capitalize# laid, so the day started right.
line @1 sympathise : Oh, come here. That's a rotten thing to happen to anyone. | That's hard, that is. Sit a minute and let it settle.
line @1 reassure : It'll come right. Things mostly do, given a day and a supper. | Don't fret yet. Even the hens settle down by dark.
line @1 decline : I would, truly, only I've promised my morning to somebody else. Another day? | Not today, #address#. My paws are full and my head's fuller.
line @1 accept_decline : That's all right. Another day, then. | No harm in asking, and no harm in a no.
line @1 thank : Oh, you're a good sort. Thank you kindly. | That's lovely of you. I'm all warm with it.
line @1 deflect_thanks : Get away with you. It was nothing at all. | Pfft. You'd do as much for me, and quicker.
line @1 preclose : Well, the hens won't name themselves. I'd best get on. | I could talk all day, and I nearly have. Off you go.
line @1 accept_preclose : Go on, then. I've talked your ear off. | Off with you. The rest can wait till next time.
line @1 tease [pair.band=friends] : You walk like the big grey goose when you're cross. I'm only saying. | Crumbs on your coat. Have you been feeding my hens behind my back?
line @1 tease.return : Cheek! I'll name a hen after you for that. | Ha! From you I'll allow it. Nobody else.
line @1 ack : #bc_it_is# | Mm, that's so.
rule @coop_cry
when concept=bark here.place=market here.time=dawn|day
does aside
cooldown 8h
say Forty hens in there, and I can name the lot. | Shoo, you great goose. That crust's for the hens.

rule @rain_hens
when concept=bark here.weather=rain
does aside
cooldown 8h
say Poor hens. Nothing looks sorrier than a wet hen. | Rain again. The coops will smell of wet feathers all day.

scene coop_names
when topic=animals|smalltalk
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail / assess
a : Every hen in #coops# answers to a name I gave it. Forty, and I've not muddled one yet. | #duchess.capitalize# pecked the coop-keeper this morning. Nobody's sorry. | There's a new hen in #coops#. Thin little thing, all legs. I've not named it yet.
b : Forty? How do you tell them apart? | Which one's the duchess? | What'll you call it?
a : By the combs, mostly. And the temper. The bossy ones have bent combs, always. | The big white one with the opinions. Nobody buys her; she frightens them off. | Sparrow, I think. It eats like one and it hops like one.
b : #bc_ha# | That sounds like a hen worth keeping. | Sparrow suits it.

scene geese_parade
when topic=animals|day here.outdoors=clear|overcast
does tell.self / react.self / laugh / assess
a : #geese.capitalize# came across the square this morning #march#. Stopped a cart dead. | A goose took a whole bun off a pup's paw today. Didn't even break step. | I counted the geese off the river carts. Eleven, and one of them limping.
b : Wish I'd seen that. | Cheeky thing. | Poor thing, the limping one.
a : The carter just sat and waited. You don't argue with a goose. | The pup laughed so hard the goose looked offended. #saying#. | I gave it the end of my crust. It hissed at me, so it's not that poorly.
b : #bc_true# | Ha! Bold as brass. | Hissing's a good sign in a goose.

scene egg_breakfast
when topic=food here.time=dawn|day
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail / backchannel
a : Had #egg# for breakfast. I knew which hen laid it, too. | I fried two eggs this morning and ate them standing at the window. Best meal of the week. | The coop-keeper gave me a cracked egg for nothing. Cracked eggs taste the same, you know.
b : You knew the hen? | Standing up? | Lucky you.
a : #brownhen.capitalize#. It's always her eggs that come out brown and round. | It tastes better standing. Don't ask me why; it does. | Lucky me. I'd have paid, and the hen would never know.
b : #bc_lovely# | I'll take your word. | #bc_lucky_you#

scene wet_coops
when topic=weather here.weather=rain
does tell.complaint / sympathise / assess
a : The hens hate this. They stand under the cart with their feathers flat and look at me like it's my fault. | Rain in the coops all morning. #feathers.capitalize#, and all of them wet. | Somebody left the coop lid off last night. Three hens soaked to the skin.
b : They'll dry. | Poor creatures. | That's careless.
a : They will, and they'll grumble till they do. Same as me. | I put a sack over the worst of it. Best I could do. | It was. I'd a word with the coop-keeper. A quiet one.

scene market_morning
when topic=day here.day=market
does tell.feeling / react.self / assess
a : Market day. Stalls up, hens out, and everybody shouting. My favourite noise. | I love a market morning. You hear everything twice before the bell. | Market day, and the egg stall sold out before I got my coat on.
b : It's a racket. | Twice? | That's early.
a : A happy racket, though. Nobody's ever cross on a market morning. | Once from whoever saw it, once from whoever heard about it. | Early is the only time for eggs. I'll know next week.

scene yard_dream
when topic=dreams|home
does confide / react.self / assess
a : One day I'll have #yard#. Six hens, no more. I'd know them better than my own kin. | I'd like a coop under my window. Just to hear them in the mornings. | If I ever had a bit of ground, I'd have a goose on it. One goose. To keep the cats honest.
b : Six is a good number. | You'd never sleep. | A goose'd keep anyone honest.
a : Six and a bench. That's all I'd want from life. | I'd not want to. Hens at dawn is better than sleep. | That's the idea. Nobody argues with a goose.

scene fox_fear
when topic=animals|crime here.time=dusk|night
does tell.worry / reassure / assess
a : Somebody said they saw #fox# by the river carts. I don't like to think of it near the coops. | I always check the coop latches at dusk. Twice, if I've heard talk of foxes. | Foxes frighten me more than robbers do. A robber doesn't kill for fun.
b : The coops are well latched. | Twice is sensible. | It'll not come into the city.
a : They are. I've checked. I'll check again on my way past. | Better twice than sorry once. #oath#, listen to me. | I'll hope you're right. I'll still check the latches.

scene parent_hens
when topic=family self.parent=yes
does tell.feeling / react.self / assess
a : I'd like #self.child# to know every hen's name, same as me. Worth knowing, names. | I'd want #self.child# to grow up liking hens. And geese. Mostly hens. | I've told #self.child# about the duchess so often, it feels like family.
b : Names are a fine thing to hand down. | Hens over geese, then. | The duchess would like that.
a : There are. Names are free and they stay with you. | Always. Hens don't hiss at you for a crust. | The duchess thinks everybody is family. Or a servant.

scene gossip_good
when topic=gossip topic.claim_kind=marriage|newcomer|promise_kept|other
does tell.gossip / react.news / assess
a : The coops had it first: #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. The hens took it well. | Word at the stalls is #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. I've told three people already. | They're saying #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. Nothing goes with eggs like news.
b : Did they, now. | Three already? | #bc_so_they_say#
a : They did. #speckle.capitalize# clucked about it all morning, none of it kind. | Four with you. I'm not proud. Well, a bit. | So they say, and I'll say it too.

scene gossip_hard
when topic=gossip|crime topic.claim_kind=death|violence|theft|victim
does tell.gossip / sympathise / assess
a : I wish I'd not heard it. #topic.subject.capitalize# #topic.claim#. | Sad word at the stalls today. #topic.subject.capitalize# #topic.claim#. | Even the hens were quiet this morning. #topic.subject.capitalize# #topic.claim#, they say.
b : That's a heavy thing to hear. | A sad day, then. | That's hard on everyone.
a : It is. I've not chattered half so much since. | It is. I'll not be laughing much today. | Heavy, yes. Gently, when I pass it on.

scene lean_eggs
when topic=times|prices world.times=hard|lean
does tell.worry / react.self / assess
a : Eggs cost more than they did, and folk are buying one where they'd buy six. | The coop-keeper's selling hens for the pot. It's lean, I understand that. Still, I hate it. | I gave my egg away this morning to somebody who needed it more.
b : It'll turn. | You can't stop it. | Kind, that.
a : It will. Hens keep laying whatever the prices do. | I can't. I can say their names when they go, though. | It was only an egg. There'll be another tomorrow.

story fox_night [self.age>=30]
  : Years back, a fox got into a yard by the river, and I heard the hens from three streets off.
  : I ran down in my nightclothes with a broom. The fox stared at me, and I stared at the fox.
  ask : What do you think the fox did?
  b : Ran off, I'd guess, with the hen in its mouth.
  b[old] : Ran. They always do, if you shout loud enough.
  : Ran, and with nothing. There I was, broom in paw, laughing till I cried. The neighbours still won't let me forget it.

story first_name [self.age>=20]
  : The first hen I ever named was a scruffy grey thing nobody would buy.
  : I called it lump, and it followed me round the coop like a pup.
  b : What became of it?
  : It lived to be very old and very rude. #saying#.

dlg @1 resident.hub [topic.meeting=stranger] : #greet#. If you're passing the coops, the big white one's the duchess. Don't let her bully you.
dlg @2 resident.hub [topic.meeting=known] : #greet#, #topic.player#. Come and say hello to the hens; they've missed you. I have too.
dlg @3 resident.hub [pair.disposition=hostile|wary] : #greet#. You look like a hen in the rain. Something wrong?
dlg @1 resident.bye : #farewell#. Mind the geese on the way out; they've no manners.
graft resident.hub
choice Do the hens in the market really all have names? -> hen_list
node hen_list
say Every one. The duchess, old speckle, sparrow, the little brown one. Forty of them. I'd tell you all forty, if you've the afternoon.
mood pleased
free
choice Which one's your favourite? -> hen_best
choice Goodbye. -> bye
node hen_best
say The little brown one with the bent comb. Never makes a fuss, lays every day, and looks at you like it knows things.
mood warm
choice That's a good hen. -> hub
choice Goodbye. -> bye


young
line @1 greet.return : #greet#! I named a new hen today. | #greet#. I've got crumbs, if you want to see the hens.
line @1 farewell.return : Bye! Say bye to the hens for me. | Bye. Don't tread on any feathers.
line @1 react.news.new : Really? I'm going to tell the hens. | #oath#! Nobody told me that.
line @1 answer.how : Good! The coop-keeper let me hold the speckled one. | Hungry. I gave my crust to the hens again.
line @1 thank : Thank you! I'll name a hen after you. | Thanks. That's the nicest thing all day.
line @1 decline : I can't, I've got to be home before the lamps. Sorry. | Better not. I'd get told off, and I'd deserve it.
rule @pup_hens
when concept=bark
does aside
cooldown 8h
say I know every hen in there. Every one! | Shoo, goose! That's my crust!

scene pup_hen_names
when topic=animals|smalltalk
does tell.boast / react.self / assess
a : I know all the hens' names. Even the new one. I named it myself. | The big white hen is called the duchess. I named her. She likes me best. | I had a crumb and nine hens came running. Nine!
b : What did you call it? | Does she? | Nine's a lot of hens.
a : Pebble. Because it's small and grey and it sits very still. | She doesn't peck me. That means she likes me. | It was a big crumb. They knew.

scene pup_goose_chase
when topic=animals|day here.outdoors=clear|overcast
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : A goose chased me right across the square. I ran so fast. | I tried to stroke a goose and it hissed like a kettle. | The geese walked past in a line, and I walked behind them, and nobody noticed.
b : Did it catch you? | They do that. | Somebody noticed, I bet.
a : No! I'm faster than a goose. Nearly. | I'll try again when it's in a better mood. | Only the last goose. It looked back at me.

scene pup_warm_egg
when topic=food
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : I found an egg in the straw and it was still warm. Warm! | I'm allowed to crack the eggs for supper now. I only dropped one. | I had a boiled egg and I dipped bread in it. That's the best supper.
b : Did you keep it? | Only one? | The very best.
a : I gave it to the coop-keeper. It wasn't mine. I held it first, though. | It was a small one. It doesn't count. | I'd have it every day if I could.

scene pup_mother_coop
when topic=family|home self.mother
does tell.complaint / react.self / assess
a : #self.mother# says I can't bring a hen home. Not even a small one. | I asked #self.mother# for a goose and got laughed at. | #self.mother# says I smell of hens. I don't think that's bad.
b : Hens are a lot of work. | A goose is a lot of goose. | It's a warm sort of smell.
a : I'd do it all. I'd feed it every day. | I'd keep it in the yard. It'd be a good goose. | That's what I said. Nobody listened.

scene pup_rain
when topic=weather here.weather=rain
does tell.feeling / react.self / assess
a : The hens hate the rain. They go all flat and cross. | I put my coat over the coop. Now I'm wet and they're dry. | It's raining, so the geese are happy and the hens are grumpy.
b : They'll dry out. | That was kind. | Geese like anything wet.
a : I know. I still feel sorry for them. | Kind and wet. I'll dry. | Geese like everything. Except me.

story pup_lost_hen
  : One day a hen got out of the coops and ran under the bread stall.
  : Everybody was chasing it and shouting, and it just sat there eating crumbs.
  ask : Do you know how I got it out?
  b : You gave it more crumbs?
  : I sat down and called it by its name, and it came. Names work. Everybody laughed, but I was right.

dlg @4 resident.hub : #greet#! Do you want to know the hens' names? I know all of them.
dlg @1 resident.job : I haven't got a job. I know all the hens' names, though. That's nearly a job.

adolescent
line @1 greet.return : #greet#. Don't mind me, I'm off to the coops. | #greet#. I've an errand, but I can talk while I walk.
line @1 farewell.return : Bye, then. Mind the geese. | See you round the stalls.
line @1 react.news.new : Who said? I'll want to hear it all. | #oath#. That hasn't reached the coops yet.
line @1 answer.how : Fine. Busy. Errands all morning. | Well enough. The coop-keeper's letting me help now.
rule @teen_coops
when concept=bark here.time=dawn|day
does aside
cooldown 8h
say Mind the coops, they've only just settled. | I'll feed them. Go on, I said I'll do it.

scene teen_helping
when topic=work|day here.time=dawn|day
does tell.boast / react.self / assess
a : The coop-keeper lets me clean the coops now. Mucky work, but they trust me with the latches. | I fed all forty hens this morning without being asked. | I sold six eggs at the stall today and gave the right change every time.
b : Trust's worth having. | Without being asked? | Every time?
a : It is. I check them twice. Nobody's lost a hen on my watch. | Without being asked. I'm practically grown. | Every time. I counted it back twice, slowly. Nobody can say I didn't.

scene teen_teased
when topic=friends|smalltalk
does tell.complaint / reassure / assess
a : My friends call me the hen pup. I'm nearly grown, I'll have them know. | Somebody clucked at me in the street. I clucked back. Louder. | They laugh because I know the hens' names. I know their names too, and that's no different.
b : They'll stop when they're bored. | Good for you. | They'll grow out of it.
a : Let's hope. I'm not stopping, so they'll have to. | It was a very good cluck. The stall laughed. | Maybe. I'll still be naming hens when they do.

scene teen_cooking
when topic=food|home
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : I made supper for everyone last night. Eggs, three ways. Nobody was sick. | I'm learning to make a pie. The crust is still like a roof tile. | I boiled an egg exactly right this morning. Runny middle, no cracks.
b : That's a fair start. | Roof tiles keep the rain off. | That's harder than it sounds.
a : I'd call it a triumph, but fair will do. | Ha. That's the nicest thing anybody's said about it. | It is. I've cracked about a hundred learning it.

dlg @5 resident.hub : #greet#. If you're after the coops, I'll show you. If you're after anything else, ask someone older.
